{"id":"ltc-trend-pivot-check","title":"Do the State's summary tables agree with its complete nursing-home data sets?","question":"The State publishes the same reports three ways (complete data sets, yearly pivot profiles, a 2019-2023 trend pivot). Do the figures agree home by home?","finding":"Net income agrees to the dollar for 94% of homes in the 2019 trend pivot, 98% of homes in the 2020 trend pivot, 74% of homes in the 2021 trend pivot, 73% of homes in the 2022 trend pivot, 91% of homes in the 2023 trend pivot; 96% in the 2015 pivot, 96% in the 2016 pivot, 96% in the 2017 pivot, 98% in the 2018 pivot, 91% in the 2023 pivot, 98% in the 2024 pivot, 96% in the 2025 pivot. 36 Bay Area home-years differ by $1 million or more in net income or in the net related-party balance; in 35 of them the summary table holds one report where the complete set holds two or more (a change of operator in the year). The four Santa Clara homes whose operator changed on 1 August 2025 are the largest: the 2025 pivot keeps the outgoing operator's balance, the complete set the incoming one's.","how":"Each pivot row (summed across a home's reports in a year) set against the complete data set for the same State facility number and report year: net income (NET_INCOME against page 8 line 255) and net related-party balance (the four balance-sheet lines). A match is a difference of $1 or less.","limits":"The tables were extracted at different dates: the trend pivot and the submitted-year pivots can carry a report before audit or revision, and a home with two reports in a year (a change of operator) may be split differently. A difference is a reason to say which file a figure came from, not a sign of misreporting.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"home","t":"Nursing home","type":"org"},{"k":"county","t":"County","type":"text"},{"k":"year","t":"Year","type":"year"},{"k":"file","t":"Summary file","type":"text"},{"k":"niComplete","t":"Net income, complete set","type":"usd"},{"k":"niPivot","t":"Net 